_base_single_iteration`: change_base(5, 8) = \"5\" \u2713\n\n`test_change_base_zero_skips_loop`: \"\" \u2713\n`test_change_base_negative_skips_loop`: \"\" \u2713\n\nRound-trip test: for bases \u2264 10, str(x % base) is a single digit 0-9, so int(result, base) == x works. Let me verify: x=999, base=8: 999 = 1740 in octal? 999 // 8 = 124, 999 % 8 = 7; 124 % 8 = 4, 124//8=15; 15%8=7, 15//8=1; 1%8=1. So digits: 7,4,7,1 \u2192 \"1747\". int(\"1747\", 8) = 1*512 + 7*64 + 4*8 + 7 = 512+448+32+7 = 999 \u2713.\n\nx=42, base=2: 101010, int = 42 \u2713.\n\nSo I'll fix the hex test to expect \"1515\" and adjust the round-trip test to only use bases \u2264 10. Also maybe add a test for base > 10 behavior documenting the digit-as-decimal behavior.\n\nLet me also double check base 36 case: change_base(31337, 36): 31337 % 36 = ? 36*870 = 31320, 31337-31320 = 17. So digit \"17\", x = 870. 870 % 36 = 870 - 24*36 = 870-864 = 6, digit \"6\", x = 24. 24 % 36 = 24, digit \"24\", x = 0. Result \"24617\". int(\"24617\", 36) would be huge, not 31337. So remove base 36 from round-trip.\n\nFinal test script:\n\n```python\n\"\"\"Tests for f044.change_base.\n\nThe function is pure (no I/O, no global state), so no monkeypatching or\nteardown is required; each test simply calls it and asserts on the result.\n\"\"\"\n\nimport pytest\n\nfrom f044 import change_base\n\n\ndef test_change_base_multi_digit_binary():\n    # x > 0 with several loop iterations: exercises lines 3-7 (loop body runs).\n    assert change_base(10, 2) == \"1010\"\n\n\ndef test_change_base_base_greater_than_ten():\n    # Digits are emitted as plain decimal numbers (str(x % base)), so base 16\n    # yields \"1515\" for 255, not \"ff\".\n    assert change_base(255, 16) == \"1515\"\n\n\ndef test_change_base_single_iteration():\n    # x < base: exactly one pass through the loop body.\n    assert change_base(5, 8) == \"5\"\n\n\ndef test_change_base_decimal():\n    assert change_base(1234, 10) == \"1234\"\n\n\ndef test_change_base_ze